; iron + carbon (0.02% to 2.1% by weight). the most important structural material.
; carbon atoms occupy interstitial sites in the iron crystal lattice.
; this distorts the lattice → dislocations cannot move as freely → harder, stronger.
; 
; grades by carbon content:
; low carbon (mild) steel:    0.02-0.3% C. ductile, weldable. structural beams.
; medium carbon steel:        0.3-0.6% C. stronger. gears, rails.
; high carbon steel:          0.6-2.1% C. very hard. cutting tools, springs.
; cast iron:                  >2.1% C. brittle but hard. engine blocks.
; 
; stainless steel: Fe + Cr (>10.5%) + Ni.
; chromium forms Cr2O3 passive layer — self-healing barrier to corrosion.
; austenitic (304, 316): non-magnetic. surgical instruments, kitchen.
; martensitic: magnetic. knife blades.
; 
; heat treatment changes microstructure and properties:
; annealing: slow cool → soft, ductile.
; quenching: rapid cool → hard, brittle (martensite).
; tempering: quench then moderate reheat → hard but less brittle.
; 
; coefficient of restitution: ~0.65-0.7 (steel ball on steel surface).

(kosha steel
  (vishesa composites-varga)
  (swarupa alloy conductor metallic)
  (yukta iron carbon ferromagnetic)
